-- p_displacement_current (7 easy, 7 medium, 7 hard = 21)
-- ============================================================
('Displacement current was introduced by', 'Ampere', 'Faraday', 'Maxwell', 'Gauss', 'option3', 'Maxwell added the displacement current term $\epsilon_0 d\Phi_E/dt$ to Ampere''s law to make it consistent.', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Displacement current arises due to', 'A time-varying electric field', 'A steady electric field', 'A magnetic field', 'Moving charges', 'option1', '$I_d = \epsilon_0 d\Phi_E/dt$. It exists even in vacuum where there are no free charges.', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electromagnetic waves are', 'Mechanical waves', 'Longitudinal waves', 'Transverse waves consisting of oscillating electric and magnetic fields', 'Stationary waves', 'option3', 'EM waves have $\vec{E}$ and $\vec{B}$ oscillating perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation.', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of electromagnetic waves in vacuum is', '$1/\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0} = 3 \times 10^8\;\text{m/s}$', '$\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0}$', '$\mu_0\epsilon_0$', '$3 \times 10^6\;\text{m/s}$', 'option1', '$c = 1/\sqrt{\mu_0\epsilon_0} \approx 3 \times 10^8\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In an electromagnetic wave, $\vec{E}$ and $\vec{B}$ are', 'Parallel to the direction of propagation', 'Parallel to each other', 'Perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation', 'At $45°$ to each other', 'option3', 'EM waves are transverse: $\vec{E} \perp \vec{B} \perp \vec{k}$ (direction of propagation).', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electromagnetic waves do not require', 'Electric and magnetic fields', 'Oscillating charges for generation', 'Energy', 'A material medium for propagation', 'option4', 'EM waves can propagate through vacuum, unlike mechanical waves.', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ratio $E_0/B_0$ in an electromagnetic wave in vacuum equals', '$1/c$', '$c$ (speed of light)', '$\mu_0$', '$\epsilon_0$', 'option2', '$E_0/B_0 = c = 3 \times 10^8\;\text{m/s}$.', 'p_displacement_current', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The displacement current between the plates of a parallel plate capacitor being charged at rate $dQ/dt$ is', '$dQ/dt$ (same as the conduction current in the wire)', 'Zero', '$\epsilon_0 dQ/dt$', '$dQ/(dt \times \epsilon_0)$', 'option1', '$I_d = \epsilon_0 dE/dt \times A = \epsilon_0 d(Q/(\epsilon_0 A))/dt \times A = dQ/dt$. It equals the conduction current.', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy density of an electromagnetic wave is', '$\frac{1}{2}\epsilon_0 E^2 + \frac{1}{2}B^2/\mu_0$', '$\epsilon_0 E^2$', '$B^2/\mu_0$', '$EB/\mu_0$', 'option1', 'Total energy density $= \frac{1}{2}\epsilon_0 E^2 + \frac{B^2}{2\mu_0}$. In an EM wave, both parts are equal.', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The intensity of an electromagnetic wave is', '$\frac{1}{2}c\epsilon_0 E_0^2$', '$cE_0 B_0$', '$E_0^2/(2\mu_0 c)$', 'All of the above', 'option4', '$I = \frac{1}{2}c\epsilon_0 E_0^2 = \frac{E_0 B_0}{2\mu_0} = \frac{E_0^2}{2\mu_0 c}$. All expressions are equivalent.', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The Poynting vector $\vec{S}$ represents', 'The rate of energy flow per unit area in an EM wave', 'The electric field strength', 'The magnetic flux', 'The displacement current', 'option1', '$\vec{S} = \frac{1}{\mu_0}\vec{E} \times \vec{B}$. It gives the direction and magnitude of energy flow.', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An EM wave has $E_0 = 30\;\text{V/m}$. The amplitude of the magnetic field is', '$30\;\text{T}$', '$10^{-8}\;\text{T}$', '$9 \times 10^9\;\text{T}$', '$10^{-7}\;\text{T}$', 'option4', '$B_0 = E_0/c = 30/(3 \times 10^8) = 10^{-7}\;\text{T}$.', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Maxwell''s equations in their complete form include', 'Coulomb''s law and Biot-Savart law', 'Only Gauss''s law and Faraday''s law', 'Only Ampere''s law', 'Gauss''s law for $E$, Gauss''s law for $B$, Faraday''s law, and Ampere-Maxwell law', 'option4', 'The four Maxwell equations unify electricity and magnetism and predict EM waves.', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Electromagnetic waves carry', 'Neither energy nor momentum', 'Only energy', 'Only momentum', 'Both energy and momentum', 'option4', 'EM waves carry energy (intensity $= P/A$) and momentum ($p = U/c$ for absorbed radiation).', 'p_displacement_current', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The radiation pressure exerted by an EM wave of intensity $I$ on a perfectly absorbing surface is', '$2I/c$', '$I/c$', '$Ic$', '$I/c^2$', 'option2', 'For perfect absorption: pressure $= I/c$. For perfect reflection: pressure $= 2I/c$.', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An EM wave of frequency $10^{10}\;\text{Hz}$ has wavelength', '$3\;\text{cm}$', '$3\;\text{m}$', '$3\;\text{mm}$', '$30\;\text{cm}$', 'option1', '$\lambda = c/f = 3 \times 10^8/10^{10} = 3 \times 10^{-2}\;\text{m} = 3\;\text{cm}$.', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The momentum delivered by an EM wave of energy $U$ to a perfectly reflecting surface is', '$U/c^2$', '$U/c$', '$2U/c$', '$Uc$', 'option3', 'For reflection, momentum change $= 2p = 2U/c$ (momentum reverses direction).', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An accelerating charge radiates EM waves. The power radiated is proportional to', 'The acceleration', 'The square of the acceleration', 'The velocity', 'The charge only', 'option2', 'Larmor formula: $P = q^2 a^2/(6\pi\epsilon_0 c^3) \propto a^2$.', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In a region of space, $\vec{E} = E_0\sin(kz - \omega t)\hat{x}$. The magnetic field is', '$\vec{B} = (E_0/c)\sin(kz - \omega t)\hat{y}$', '$\vec{B} = (E_0/c)\sin(kz - \omega t)\hat{z}$', '$\vec{B} = E_0 c\sin(kz - \omega t)\hat{y}$', '$\vec{B} = (E_0/c)\cos(kz - \omega t)\hat{y}$', 'option1', 'Wave propagates along $\hat{z}$, $\vec{E}$ along $\hat{x}$. $\vec{B}$ must be along $\hat{y}$ (since $\vec{E} \times \vec{B} \parallel \hat{z}$). $B_0 = E_0/c$, same phase.', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The speed of EM waves in a medium of relative permittivity $\epsilon_r$ and relative permeability $\mu_r$ is', '$c\sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}$', '$c/\sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}$', '$c/(\mu_r\epsilon_r)$', '$c\mu_r\epsilon_r$', 'option2', '$v = 1/\sqrt{\mu\epsilon} = 1/\sqrt{\mu_r\mu_0\epsilon_r\epsilon_0} = c/\sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}$.', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The average energy density of an EM wave with $E_{rms} = 100\;\text{V/m}$ is approximately', '$8.85 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{J/m}^3$', '$4.43 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{J/m}^3$', '$1.77 \times 10^{-7}\;\text{J/m}^3$', '$8.85 \times 10^{-5}\;\text{J/m}^3$', 'option1', '$u = \epsilon_0 E_{rms}^2 = 8.85 \times 10^{-12} \times 10^4 = 8.85 \times 10^{-8}\;\text{J/m}^3$ (total, since electric and magnetic parts are equal).', 'p_displacement_current', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),

