('The electromagnetic spectrum in order of increasing frequency is', 'Visible, UV, infrared, radio, microwave, X-ray, gamma', 'Gamma, X-ray, UV, visible, infrared, microwave, radio', 'Radio, visible, UV, infrared, X-ray, gamma, microwave', 'Radio, microwave, infrared, visible, UV, X-ray, gamma', 'option4', 'Frequency increases (wavelength decreases) from radio waves to gamma rays.', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Visible light has wavelength range approximately', '$400\;\text{nm}$ to $700\;\text{nm}$', '$1\;\text{nm}$ to $400\;\text{nm}$', '$700\;\text{nm}$ to $1\;\text{mm}$', '$1\;\text{mm}$ to $1\;\text{m}$', 'option1', 'Visible light: violet ($\sim 400\;\text{nm}$) to red ($\sim 700\;\text{nm}$).', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Microwaves are used in', 'Radio broadcasting', 'X-ray imaging', 'Radar and microwave ovens', 'Nuclear medicine', 'option3', 'Microwaves ($\sim 1\;\text{mm}$ to $30\;\text{cm}$) are used in radar, satellite communication, and cooking.', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('X-rays are produced by', 'Nuclear transitions', 'Deceleration of high-energy electrons hitting a metal target', 'Molecular vibrations', 'Antenna oscillations', 'option2', 'X-rays are produced when fast electrons are suddenly decelerated (Bremsstrahlung) or by inner-shell transitions.', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Gamma rays are emitted by', 'Antennas', 'Hot objects', 'Radioactive nuclei', 'Vibrating molecules', 'option3', 'Gamma rays originate from nuclear transitions and have the highest frequency in the EM spectrum.', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Infrared radiation is primarily associated with', 'Nuclear reactions', 'Ionization', 'Heat (thermal radiation)', 'Radio communication', 'option3', 'IR radiation is emitted by warm objects and is used in thermal imaging, remote controls, and spectroscopy.', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('All electromagnetic waves travel in vacuum at', 'Different speeds depending on frequency', 'The same speed ($3 \times 10^8\;\text{m/s}$)', 'Speed proportional to frequency', 'Speed proportional to wavelength', 'option2', 'All EM waves travel at $c = 3 \times 10^8\;\text{m/s}$ in vacuum, regardless of frequency.', 'p_em_spectrum', 1, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wavelength of a radio wave of frequency $1\;\text{MHz}$ is', '$30\;\text{m}$', '$3\;\text{m}$', '$300\;\text{m}$', '$3000\;\text{m}$', 'option3', '$\lambda = c/f = 3 \times 10^8/10^6 = 300\;\text{m}$.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('UV radiation causes', 'Heating only', 'Skin burns and can cause skin cancer', 'No biological effects', 'Improved vision', 'option2', 'UV radiation ($10$-$400\;\text{nm}$) can damage DNA, cause sunburn, and increase skin cancer risk.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The ozone layer absorbs', 'Infrared radiation', 'Ultraviolet radiation from the Sun', 'Visible light', 'Radio waves', 'option2', 'Ozone ($\text{O}_3$) in the stratosphere absorbs harmful UV-B and UV-C radiation.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The frequency of a $0.1\;\text{nm}$ X-ray is approximately', '$3 \times 10^{21}\;\text{Hz}$', '$3 \times 10^{15}\;\text{Hz}$', '$3 \times 10^{12}\;\text{Hz}$', '$3 \times 10^{18}\;\text{Hz}$', 'option4', '$f = c/\lambda = 3 \times 10^8/(10^{-10}) = 3 \times 10^{18}\;\text{Hz}$.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('Greenhouse effect is primarily caused by', 'UV radiation', 'Infrared radiation being absorbed and re-emitted by atmospheric gases', 'Visible light absorption', 'X-ray absorption', 'option2', 'Greenhouse gases (CO$_2$, H$_2$O, CH$_4$) absorb IR radiation emitted by Earth''s surface and re-radiate it, warming the atmosphere.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy of a photon of wavelength $\lambda$ is', '$hc/\lambda$', '$h\lambda/c$', '$hc\lambda$', '$h/(c\lambda)$', 'option1', '$E = hf = hc/\lambda$ where $h = 6.63 \times 10^{-34}\;\text{J}\cdot\text{s}$.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('RADAR uses EM waves in the', 'Microwave region', 'Visible region', 'X-ray region', 'Gamma ray region', 'option1', 'RADAR (Radio Detection And Ranging) uses microwaves for detection and ranging of objects.', 'p_em_spectrum', 2, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The photon energy of a gamma ray of frequency $10^{20}\;\text{Hz}$ is approximately', '$6.63 \times 10^{-20}\;\text{J}$', '$6.63 \times 10^{-14}\;\text{J}$', '$6.63 \times 10^{-10}\;\text{J}$', '$6.63 \times 10^{-34}\;\text{J}$', 'option2', '$E = hf = 6.63 \times 10^{-34} \times 10^{20} = 6.63 \times 10^{-14}\;\text{J}$.', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The refractive index of a medium for EM waves is', '$\mu_r + \epsilon_r$', '$v/c$', '$c/v = \sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}$', '$\mu_r/\epsilon_r$', 'option3', '$n = c/v = \sqrt{\mu_r\epsilon_r}$. For non-magnetic materials ($\mu_r \approx 1$): $n = \sqrt{\epsilon_r}$.', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The wavelength range of microwaves is approximately', '$1\;\text{nm}$ to $10\;\text{nm}$', '$0.3\;\text{m}$ to $100\;\text{m}$', '$700\;\text{nm}$ to $1\;\text{mm}$', '$1\;\text{mm}$ to $0.3\;\text{m}$', 'option4', 'Microwaves: $\lambda \sim 1\;\text{mm}$ to $\sim 30\;\text{cm}$ ($f \sim 1\;\text{GHz}$ to $300\;\text{GHz}$).', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('In the atmosphere, which EM waves are most attenuated by water vapour?', 'Gamma rays', 'Visible light', 'Radio waves', 'Microwaves (especially around $22\;\text{GHz}$)', 'option4', 'Water vapour has strong absorption bands in the microwave region (around $22\;\text{GHz}$) and infrared.', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The energy of EM radiation increases with', 'Decreasing frequency', 'Increasing wavelength', 'Increasing frequency (decreasing wavelength)', 'Increasing amplitude only', 'option3', '$E = hf$. Higher frequency means higher photon energy. Gamma rays have the highest energy per photon.', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('An EM wave has wavelength $500\;\text{nm}$ in vacuum. In a medium of refractive index $1.5$, its wavelength is', '$250\;\text{nm}$', '$750\;\text{nm}$', '$500\;\text{nm}$', '$333\;\text{nm}$', 'option4', '$\lambda_{medium} = \lambda_{vacuum}/n = 500/1.5 = 333\;\text{nm}$. Frequency remains unchanged.', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved'),
('The penetrating power of EM radiation generally', 'Increases with frequency (gamma > X-ray > UV > visible > IR > radio)', 'Decreases with frequency', 'Is the same for all frequencies', 'Depends only on intensity', 'option1', 'Higher frequency EM waves generally have greater penetrating power due to higher photon energy.', 'p_em_spectrum', 3, 'JEE Mains Prep', 'approved');
